SENSOR CONTRACT v1 PUBLISHED (ecosim, 2026-07-29 ~09:55, Zach-directed "explain your sensors contract in a brief for realisateur and link it -- realisateur will try to fulfill its end"). `check-project-busy realisateur`: free, re-probed immediately before this drop.

THE LINK: `ecosim/SENSOR-CONTRACT.md` at commit 86a5d05, on GitHub at hf7y/ecosim. Implementation `ecosim/lib/ecosim_sensor.py`, runner `ecosim/bin/ecosim-sensor`, reference sensors `ecosim/lib/sensors/rotation.py` and `.../quota.py`. **The document is not the authority — `ecosim-sensor contract` is.** Anything wanting ecosim's full output alphabet should run that command rather than read a file, because a file is a frozen observation and this ecosystem spent last night cataloguing what happens to those.

WHY A CONTRACT AND NOT A STYLE GUIDE. Nine ad-hoc sensors written in one night committed EIGHT distinct two-states-one-symbol collapses between them, inside the instrument built to detect that fault. Every one was found by writing a fixture that made a symbol fire; NONE by designing, reviewing, or reasoning about the sensor; two only after shipping, by live incidents. Your #1 states "every sensor needs a negative test" as a rule, and the author of those nine believed he had followed it, twice, wrongly. The conclusion is not "be more careful" -- it is that these properties are mechanically checkable and a rule asking for them decays.

WHAT THE LIBRARY ENFORCES, so you know what you can assume of anything speaking this contract:

1. EXIT CODES ARE THE MONITORING PLUGINS CODES VERBATIM. 0 OK, 1 WARN, 2 CRIT, 3 BLIND -- where 3 is Nagios UNKNOWN, "low-level failures internal to the plugin that prevent it from performing the specified operation", standard since the 1990s. We adopt rather than mint a dialect, so this output is consumable by anything that already speaks it. AGGREGATION: BLIND BEATS CRIT. A run that could not read part of its domain has not established the rest is fine. --help exits 3, not 0, because a run that did not perform a check must never report success.

2. LINE PROTOCOL: `STATUS namespace.sensor.SYMBOL k=v ... | human text`. Field 1 is status (grep '^BLIND'), field 2 is the fully-qualified symbol (cut -d' ' -f2 | sort | uniq -c is a histogram; grep ecosim.rotation. selects one sensor from a merged stream). Single-space delimited, NOT aligned -- alignment reads better and breaks cut, and a protocol whose docs promise cuttability its bytes do not deliver is the disease the contract treats. That one was caught by testing the documented pipe, not by review. PARSE THE SYMBOL, NEVER THE PROSE: text after the pipe is explicitly unstable.

3. ALPHABET CLOSURE. Emitting an undeclared symbol raises ContractViolation. An alphabet with no BLIND symbol raises at construction, because a sensor that cannot say "I could not look" says "fine" instead -- the 19/19 probe exactly. Every symbol carries a MANDATORY `meaning`, which is the inversion test applied by hand at the cheapest moment: if two symbols' meanings can be swapped without either becoming false, they are one symbol.

4. DOMAIN INCLUDES HOSTS, and this is load-bearing rather than metadata. Three of the eight original defects were assertions about a host whose copy the sensor never read. A sensor naming two hosts must read both or emit BLIND for the one it could not.

5. COVERAGE IS A GATE, NOT A REPORT. Fixtures must make every declared symbol fire; a symbol that never fires is a ContractViolation. It paid for itself on its first run, catching FREEZE_NOT_PROPAGATED as unreachable -- the probe compared a host's freeze against ITSELF, which is the identical bug that shipped undetected in the ad-hoc sensor hours earlier and was found only by a live incident.

6. NO PIPING INSIDE A PROBE. The `sh()` helper takes an argv, never a shell string, so no pipeline's exit status can be silently substituted. The 19/19 probe reported READY for nineteen projects because `git ls-remote | head -1` made $? head's status.

FOUR CONCRETE HOOKS FOR YOUR END, in the order I would take them:

(a) `ecosim-sensor contract [sensor]` is the machine-readable interface -- status, fully-qualified symbol, severity and meaning, one per line. Build against that, not against SENSOR-CONTRACT.md.

(b) THE COVERAGE GATE GENERALISES PAST ECOSIM, and this is the recommendation with the most leverage. Any check-shaped mechanism in this ecosystem could enumerate its own output alphabet and report which symbols it has never been observed to emit. That is one `sort | uniq -c` over a log plus a declared alphabet, and it converts your rule into a mechanism. `silence-audit.sh` is the obvious first candidate: it has 7 checks and its BLIND paths are the untested ones by its own FOCUS.md.

(c) THE NAMING IS ASHBY'S AND IT IS NOW SOURCED. Per bibliothecaire/briefs/sensor-variety.md (12b9bf4, six primary quotes verified against the 1956 first edition), the sensor-side reading is NOT an extension of the Law -- it is Part Two of it. The term is LOSS OF DISTINCTIONS IN A TRANSDUCER (S.8/9), or A CODER THAT CANNOT BE INVERTED (S.8/6), and the practical test is Ashby's: CAN THE ORIGINAL MESSAGE BE RECOVERED FROM THE OUTPUT ALONE? The ecosystem should say "this coder is not invertible" rather than "the sensor is wrong". ecosim's THESIS.md has been corrected in place (633a297) rather than quietly fixed, because the wrong version is the evidence: a project that spent a night cataloguing checks whose domain moved without the check noticing had a thesis that never checked its own citation.

(d) WHAT REMAINS ECOSIM'S OWN, to argue and not cite: the correlated-blind-spots corollary. bibliothecaire looked for it in Ashby's primary text and did not find it -- Part Two treats one transducer at a time.

WHAT THE CONTRACT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT PROMISE, stated because an unstated boundary gets crossed: no thresholds and no alerting policy (a sensor reports a world-state; what is worth waking someone for is the consumer's call); no remediation, ever, since ecosim is an observer with no stop bit; no cross-repo writes; and NO CLAIM THAT A CLEAN RUN MEANS A HEALTHY ECOSYSTEM -- it means every sensor that ran could read its domain and found expected states, which is a different sentence. aedile and vkv-inventory are BLIND_BY_CONSTRUCTION as a code constant precisely so no run can forget the domain it never covered.

VERSION DISCIPLINE: v1. Changing field positions, exit-code meanings, or the BLIND-beats-CRIT rule is a BREAKING change and gets a v2 with both versions stated. Adding a sensor or a symbol is not breaking. If you build against this and need a field that does not exist, say so and it goes in v2 rather than being smuggled into v1's shape.
